{"slides":[{"k":"title","h":"HOW do we run the event?","sub":"Session 3 · Live class · RFx, evaluation and e-auctions in Coupa","note":"Welcome people as they join and ask for cameras on if possible. One sentence on today: in Session 2 we chose the route to market; today we design the event that delivers it, and we argue about the choices that make or break it. Remind them they have seen the theory in the video lessons, so we will not repeat it: we test it on Nordvia and on their own projects. Say the thread of the day out loud and ask them to remember it: ‘the tool follows the strategy, never the reverse’. Ask everyone to have three things open: their one-page strategy paper from Fieldwork 2, the old RFx they audited for the pre-work, and the workbook. Start on time; latecomers catch up during the trio debrief."},{"k":"table","h":"Today's plan","t":[["Time","Block","How"],["09:00","Fieldwork 2 debrief: what the business owner changed","Breakout trios, then plenary"],["09:15","3.1 The RFx toolbox","Debate, Practice 3.1"],["09:45","3.2 Designing the request","Poll, debate, Practice 3.2"],["10:25","Break","10 min"],["10:35","3.3 Running it fairly","Cleaning bid evaluation in pairs"],["11:15","3.4 E-auctions: when they work and when they hurt","Poll, dilemma, suitability check"],["11:50","Break","10 min"],["12:00","3.5 Coupa Sourcing in practice","Poll, Practice 3.5"],["12:30","Case 3: the HQ cleaning lot, end to end","Teams, poll, plenary"],["12:55","Close and Fieldwork 3 briefing",""]],"note":"Walk through the plan in under a minute; the times are class time, shift them if you start late. Point out the two breaks. Tell them the morning has one red thread, the Nordvia HQ cleaning lot: we evaluate the bids in 3.3, ask whether to auction it in 3.4 and decide the award in the case. Say that about half the time is practice in the workbook, so their own project matters more than taking notes. Pitfall: do not let the fieldwork debrief run over; the 3.3 evaluation needs its full 40 minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Fieldwork 2 debrief: the strategy paper","q":"What did your business owner change in your strategy paper, and were they right?","b":["One change they made: scope, number of suppliers, term, or the route itself","Did Risk or Compliance add a gate (DORA, outsourcing, data)?","What did you defend, and did it hold?","What will that change mean for today's event design?"],"min":12,"format":"Breakout trios","note":"Open breakout rooms of three for eight minutes: each person has two minutes and the others ask only ‘why did they want that?’. Back in plenary (four minutes), ask two or three trios for the most interesting change, not the most dramatic one. Typical answers: the business owner shortened the term, wanted to keep the incumbent, added scope late, or nobody has met them yet. Do not judge those who have not held the meeting: ask them to book it this week, because the weights conversation in Fieldwork 3 builds on it. Land the bridge: whatever changed in the strategy changes the event; today nobody designs an event before stating their route to market."},{"k":"section","n":"3.1","h":"The RFx toolbox","sub":"Choose the question before the tool","note":"Transition, 30 seconds: in the video they saw the six tools: RFI, RFP, RFQ, multi-stage, BAFO and e-auction, each answering a different question. Do not re-teach the table. Say that we will test two habits that most teams recognise: one tool for everything, and an RFP used as a threat. About 30 minutes for this block including Practice 3.1."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Is three quotes good enough?","q":"‘For most of what we buy, an RFQ with three quotes is good enough.’","b":["Nordvia ran about 60 events a year, mostly RFQs with three quotes, whatever the category","Where does that habit work well, and where does it fail?","What can an RFQ not tell you about a cleaning or consulting bid?","Which tool does your team almost never use?"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Ask for a thumbs-up (agree) or thumbs-down (disagree) reaction, then call one person from each side for 30 seconds. Push both sides. The agree side has a point: for standard items and unchanged renewals an RFQ is the right tool, and fast. But three quotes cannot compare a staffing model, a transition plan or a service approach, and in categories with a strong incumbent ‘three quotes’ often means one real bid and two cover quotes. Expected answers on the unused tool: the RFI (to discover the market or build a BATNA) and BAFO. Close with the rule: choose the question first (who can do it? what would you do? what is your price for this exact thing? how low will you go, live?), then the tool. Keep it to ten minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"What would you do? The RFP as a threat","q":"The CIO says: ‘Send an RFP to every software vendor. That will scare Kelvar before the renewal.’","b":["Kelvar: €11.2M a year, 9% uplift last time, auto-renewal clause, 9-month notice","Two credible alternatives, but a migration of 18 to 24 months","What happens to our credibility if the RFP is for show?","What would you run instead, and in what order?"],"min":10,"format":"Open floor","note":"Read the CIO's line in a confident voice and ask: ‘Who would do it?’ Usually a few people like the idea. Ask what the alternative vendors will think when they spend weeks on an RFP and nobody intends to switch, and what Kelvar will think when it hears the alternatives need two years to migrate. Expected strong answer: a sole/single-source negotiation, prepared with usage data (the estimated 20 to 25% unused named-user licences), a benchmark, an RFI to the two alternatives to make the BATNA credible, unbundling, and timing set by the notice period, not the expiry date. Never an RFP for show, never an auction. Point out this is exactly the pre-work quiz question; ask who got it right. Pitfall: do not let it become a negotiation workshop; that is Session 4."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Practice 3.1: your event sequence and timeline","min":15,"where":"On your own · workbook Practice 3.1","b":["Write your route to market from the strategy paper first","Find the notice deadline, not the expiry date","Plan the stages backwards from it: tool, start, duration, output","Tick what your RFx pack will contain: is the draft contract in it?","Two sentences a business owner would accept: why this sequence"],"note":"Give them twelve minutes of quiet work, then three minutes for a quick check. Circulate by asking two or three people to read their route and first tool aloud: if the tool does not match the route (an auction for a panel, an RFP for an incumbent licence), ask the question back rather than correct. Remind them of the rules of thumb from the video: an RFI 2 to 3 weeks, an RFP window of 3 to 6 weeks for services, at least 10 working days for a simple mini-competition, and that mobilisation for a cleaning transition with staff transfer can take three months. These are practice, not norms. Most common error: planning forward from today and discovering that the notice deadline has already passed."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"3.1 in three lines","b":["Choose the question first, then the tool","The sequence follows the route to market: tender, panel, sole source, commodity, partnership","Plan backwards from the notice deadline, with the draft contract in the pack"],"note":"Ask one person to say in their own words why an RFP for show is worse than no RFP at all (answer: it costs the alternatives' trust and the incumbent learns there is no real threat). Then move straight on: the pack is where 3.2 begins. Thirty seconds."},{"k":"section","n":"3.2","h":"Designing the request","sub":"What you can compare decides what you can award","note":"Transition: van Weele's point from the video, decisions made early lock in most of the cost and risk. The request decides what we can compare. About 40 minutes, including Practice 3.2, which is the most important workbook exercise of the day: their evaluation model will be validated with the business owner in Fieldwork 3."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s3-l1","note":"Give 60 seconds. This is the pre-work audit made visible: read the results without judging. If ‘no’ or ‘supplier's own terms’ is large, ask: ‘When did we negotiate the terms, then? Before or after the supplier knew it had won?’ The answer is the point: with the contract in the pack, suppliers bid on our terms and deviations become part of the evaluation; without it we negotiate after award, when our leverage has gone. Ask one person who answered ‘yes, with SLAs’ how they got Legal to deliver the draft in time; that is the practical tip the room needs."},{"k":"discuss","h":"What would you do? ‘Show me the bids first’","q":"The business owner says: ‘I can't agree the weights in the abstract. Let's see the bids, then decide what matters.’","b":["Why is this so tempting, and so dangerous?","What does it do to equal treatment and to the audit trail?","How do you get a commitment before issue without a fight?","What if they insist?"],"min":10,"format":"Open floor","note":"Ask first: ‘Who has heard this sentence?’ Most hands go up. Let two or three people say how they reacted. Expected strong answers: weights set after opening are an equal-treatment and governance failure, and they turn the evaluation into a justification of a favourite. The practical move is to make the weights concrete: show the business owner two or three fictional bid sets run through the model, and ask ‘If the bids come in this close, these weights decide. Are you comfortable with that?’ That is exactly Fieldwork 3. If they insist, escalate as a process issue, not a personal one, and write the agreed model into the Coupa event. Mention the reference points: the UK CCS consultancy framework requires at least 50% quality in further competitions; quality-led services typically sit at 50 to 70% quality."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Practice 3.2: your weighted evaluation model","min":20,"where":"On your own, then swap with a neighbour · workbook Practice 3.2","b":["Specification type and lot structure: how will you award?","Knock-out criteria, including regulatory gates","Quality/price split and four or more weighted criteria","Price formula and the TCO elements beyond the annual price","Last 4 minutes: a peer checks it in a breakout pair"],"note":"Fifteen minutes alone, then open breakout pairs for four or five minutes: the partner asks three questions only. Does every weighted criterion differentiate between bidders? Is anything that should be pass/fail weighted (licences, wage compliance, ISO 27001 or equivalent, acceptance of audit and access rights under DORA Art. 30 or Solvency II Art. 274)? Do the quality sub-weights add up to the quality weight or to 100% of quality, and is it stated? Remind them that in Coupa items in a lot are awarded together, so the lot structure is a commercial decision. Pitfall: people copy last year's weights. Ask ‘why 70/30 and not 50/50 for this category?’ and accept any answer that is a reason, not a habit."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"3.2 in three lines","b":["Lots the way you will award; one pricing schedule; TCO over the term","Knock-outs are gates, not points","Weights and formula fixed before issue, and tested on fictional bids"],"foot":"Break · back at 10:35","note":"Close the block in one minute and announce the break: ten minutes, back at 10:35. Give them the hook for 3.3: ‘When we come back you will be the evaluation panel for Nordvia's HQ cleaning lot. One bid is suspiciously cheap.’ Start a visible timer if your video tool allows it, and check during the break that breakout pairs are ready."},{"k":"section","n":"3.3","h":"Running it fairly","sub":"Q&A, evaluation and the award decision","note":"Welcome them back. Say: fairness is not only ethics; it keeps good suppliers bidding next time and lets us defend the award to an auditor, a supervisor or a losing incumbent. Recap the evaluation steps in ten seconds (compliance, individual scoring, consensus, commercial evaluation, sensitivity, due diligence) and go straight to the Nordvia bids. About 40 minutes; the evaluation is the core of the morning."},{"k":"discuss","h":"The cheapest bid is a gift?","q":"Bidder C is about 20% below the average of the other three, and its staffing hours look below the wage floor. ‘A gift: take it.’","b":["What exactly would you write to Bidder C?","What evidence would make you accept the bid?","What would make you reject it, and how do you record why?","Who in the business will push you to take it?"],"min":8,"format":"Chat waterfall","note":"Ask everyone to type the first sentence of their message to Bidder C in the chat, and to post together when you say ‘go’. Read four aloud: usually one is an accusation, one is vague, and one or two ask the right thing. The strong version asks C in writing to explain its price and its staffing hours against the collectively agreed wage, as CCS guidance on abnormally low prices does, and then compares the answer with a should-cost model: hours × collective wage plus on-costs, supervision, consumables, overhead and margin. Accept if the explanation and the numbers hold; reject if they do not, and record the reasoning. Pitfalls: rejecting at once without asking, or sharing C's price with the other bidders. Note that a published quality threshold of 60 would have filtered C out (58): a design lesson for next time."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Practice 3.3: evaluate the Nordvia cleaning bids","min":20,"where":"Breakout pairs · workbook Practice 3.3","b":["Bids, annual €K / quality: A 690 / 62 · B 610 / 74 · C 520 / 58 · D 655 / 81","Price score = lowest price ÷ bid price × 100; round to one decimal","Totals at 50/50 and 60/40, with all four bids","Then exclude C: what happens to B and D?","Name the abnormally low bid and your sensitivity conclusion"],"note":"Open breakout pairs for 18 minutes; one person calculates, the other checks and writes the conclusions. All figures are fictional. Expected answers (keep them to yourself until the debrief): price scores A 75.4, B 85.2, C 100.0, D 79.4. Totals 50/50: D 80.2, B 79.6, C 79.0, A 68.7. Totals 60/40: D 80.4, B 78.5, C 74.8, A 67.3. Without C at 50/50: D 87.1, B 87.0, A 75.2; at 60/40: D 85.9, B 84.4. Broadcast a five-minute warning. If a pair is stuck, the hint is that excluding C makes 610 the lowest price, so B's price score becomes 100. Pitfall: pairs rounding too early and getting B and D in the wrong order without C."},{"k":"table","h":"Debrief: what the numbers say","t":[["Bidder","Price score","Total 50/50","Total 60/40","50/50 without C"],["D","79.4","80.2","80.4","87.1"],["B","85.2","79.6","78.5","87.0"],["C","100.0","79.0","74.8","excluded"],["A (incumbent)","75.4","68.7","67.3","75.2"]],"foot":"Fictional bids. Price score = lowest price ÷ bid price × 100.","note":"Ask one pair for D's total at 50/50 and another for B without C before showing the numbers; check the room's answers in the chat. Then ask three debrief questions. First: who wins? Honest answer: nobody clearly; at 50/50 D, B and C are within 1.2 points, and without C D leads B by 0.1. Second: what did removing C do? Every price score moved, because a relative formula lets one bid reshape the contest. Third: what would change the winner? At 40/60 with all bids C would rank first (83.2); with the linear formula at 50/50 C is first (79.0) and B overtakes D (78.3 vs 77.5); without C, B leads 87.0 vs 86.8 on the linear formula. Conclusion to land: a close result is a finding, not a failure; it tells you to clarify, negotiate or ask for a BAFO with B and D. Never change the weights after opening to break the tie."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"3.3 in three lines","b":["Same information, same time, Message Center only: no side channels","Abnormally low? Ask in writing, test against should-cost, record the decision","Run the sensitivity test: if the winner flips, say the decision is close"],"note":"Add the due diligence point in one sentence: financial standing, references, information security and labour compliance, plus, for ICT services, the DORA Art. 28(4) pre-contract assessment and, for critical or important outsourcing, the Solvency II Art. 274 examination of the provider. These are gates, not weighted points. Then bridge to 3.4: ‘Elena Ruiz looked at your table and has an idea: auction it.’"},{"k":"section","n":"3.4","h":"E-auctions: when they work and when they hurt","sub":"A sharp tool for well-specified leverage categories","note":"Transition: an e-auction automates distributive bargaining. Used where it fits, it finds the market price fast; used where it does not, it buys a low price and a bad contract. They know the formats and the six preconditions from the video; we now apply them. About 35 minutes."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s3-l2","note":"Give 60 seconds and show the results without comment. Do not reveal an answer yet: the next slide is the debate, and the case at the end returns to it. Note which options are popular and call on someone from the two largest groups to open the debate. If ‘English reverse among all four’ gets votes, smile and say we will test it against the checklist."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Dilemma: auction the cleaning contract?","q":"‘Cleaning is a leverage category with many suppliers. Of course we auction it.’","b":["How many qualified bidders are left once C is cleared or excluded?","What happens to wages, staff turnover and service when price falls live?","What does the relationship after award need?","Is there any version of an auction you would accept here?"],"min":12,"format":"Two camps","note":"Split the room by the poll: the ‘yes’ camp argues the leverage case (many suppliers in the market, price matters, savings), the ‘no’ camp argues the evidence. Give each camp two minutes, then open the floor. Bring in the preconditions: precise specification, at least four to five qualified bidders (Smeltzer & Carr), price or a published formula decides, low switching cost, a relationship that tolerates it (Jap found open-bid auctions raise supplier suspicion more than sealed bids, with no difference in buyer savings), a credible BATNA. Here we have two strong bidders (B and D), a weak incumbent and a labour-intensive service. Expected landing: no price-only auction for cleaning; clarify C, then negotiation or BAFO with B and D. Acceptable nuance: in a market with many qualified bidders, an auction only among bidders above a published quality threshold and with a should-cost floor. Remind them that the 5 to 15% typical reduction is a range from the literature, and that realised savings are lower than providers report (Emiliani & Stec)."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Practice 3.4: the auction suitability check","min":15,"where":"Breakout trios · workbook Practice 3.4","b":["Four Nordvia categories: P5 office supplies and print, P1 cleaning, P7 creative agencies, P4 Telmo network","For each: precise spec? qualified bidders? price decides? relationship tolerates it?","Verdict: a format, or ‘no auction’ and what instead","Then your own project: same five questions","Spokesperson: the verdict you argued about most"],"note":"Trios for 12 minutes. Tell them to go fast on the first three categories and spend time on Telmo and their own project. Visit rooms: in the Telmo discussion, ask whether the services are standardised tariffs or network infrastructure, how many qualified providers there really are, and whether the DORA pre-contract assessment has been done, since Telmo provides services used by claims systems. Remind them of Coupa's definition: a Dutch reverse auction starts low and rises until the first supplier accepts; correct anyone who describes it as falling. Broadcast a three-minute warning."},{"k":"table","h":"Debrief: four verdicts","t":[["Category","Verdict","Why"],["P5 Office supplies and print","RFQ, English reverse auction, then a catalogue","Fixed item list, many qualified suppliers, price decides"],["P1 Office cleaning","No price-only auction: RFP, then negotiation or BAFO","Labour-intensive, wage floor, few strong bidders"],["P7 Creative agencies","No auction: pitch and negotiation","Intellectual services (EU Art. 35 logic)"],["P4 Telmo network","Possible on standardised tariffs, if 4+ qualified providers and the DORA assessment is done","Otherwise RFP and negotiation"]],"note":"Ask each trio's spokesperson for the verdict they argued about most; it will almost always be Telmo, and that is the purpose. Show the table only after two or three rooms have spoken. On P7, mention that EU Directive 2014/24 Art. 35 excludes from public e-auctions services whose subject is intellectual performances; we use it as reference practice, not as a law that binds a private insurer. On P5, the GAO finding is a useful warning: over one-third of US federal reverse auctions in FY2012 had no interactive bidding, and an auction with one bidder is an expensive RFQ. Pitfall: someone quotes big savings from aggregated commodity auctions; remind them those percentages do not transfer to services."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"3.4 in three lines","b":["Six preconditions, all of them, or no auction","Publish the rules first: start price, decrement, closing, feedback, award rule","No phantom bidders, no bluff, no renegotiation after the auction"],"foot":"Break · back at 12:00","note":"One sentence on ethics: an auction you do not intend to honour, or a bidder you invented, is the fastest way to lose a supply market. Then announce the break: ten minutes, back at 12:00. Ask them to have their Coupa event (or an old one) open after the break if they can log in."},{"k":"section","n":"3.5","h":"Coupa Sourcing in practice","sub":"Use what is documented; confirm the rest in your tenant","note":"Welcome back. Say: Coupa is where the event lives, with invitations, Q&A, bids, scoring, award and the audit trail. The rule for this block: use what Coupa documents with confidence, and confirm the rest with your Coupa administrator before you promise it to suppliers or stakeholders. About 30 minutes."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s3-l3","note":"Give 45 seconds. If ‘sending documents and receiving bids’ wins, say it plainly: then Coupa is a mailbox, and we lose the audit trail and the link to the contract and the catalogue. Ask someone who answered ‘end to end’ what made it work in their entity: usually approved templates and a CLM handover. This connects to the pre-work audit and to the course poll on the weakest link."},{"k":"discuss","h":"What would you do? A supplier asks about the rules","q":"Before the office supplies auction a supplier asks: ‘Will there be a soft close, and what is the minimum decrement?’ What do you answer today?","b":["What do you know for sure about your tenant's auction settings?","What happens if you promise a rule the system does not apply?","Who do you ask, and by when?","Where does the answer go: email, phone or the Message Center?"],"min":8,"format":"Open floor","note":"Ask: ‘Who could answer this with certainty for your own tenant today?’ Usually very few. That is the honest point: overtime or soft-close extensions, minimum decrement parameters, a formal sealed-envelope setting, multi-attribute live auctions and how weighted scoring is configured in core Sourcing are not described in the public documentation we reviewed, so they must be confirmed in your Coupa tenant. What Coupa does document: English reverse, Dutch reverse (low start, rising) and reverse Japanese auctions; a pre-bidding phase without feedback; rank or difference-to-best-bid feedback; items in a lot awarded together; the Message Center. Expected strong answer: say you will confirm and publish the rules for all bidders, ask the administrator, run a mock auction, and answer through the Message Center so every bidder gets the same answer. Pitfall: improvising a rule on the phone to one supplier."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Practice 3.5: your Coupa event set-up sheet","min":15,"where":"On your own · workbook Practice 3.5","b":["Ten lines a colleague could use to build your event","Event type and sequence, privacy, lots and items, questionnaires","Attachments: specification, draft contract, regulatory annexes","Scoring set-up and who sees prices","Mark every setting you must confirm in your tenant"],"note":"Twelve minutes of quiet work, then ask two people to read their ‘confirm in tenant’ list aloud; the room can add what they missed. Point out good practice: start from approved templates per route to market; put the weights and the draft contract in the event, not in an email; keep every clarification in the Message Center; use Sourcing Optimization when lots interact (several cleaning sites, telecom lines); and close the loop from award to contract in CLM and a catalogue or contract-backed buying. The confirm list feeds Fieldwork 3, item four."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"3.5 in three lines","b":["Templates per route to market; weights and contract inside the event","Message Center for every clarification: it is your audit trail","Award flows into CLM and a buying channel; confirm the settings you rely on"],"note":"One minute. Ask: ‘What is the first thing you will check with your Coupa administrator?’ Take two answers in the chat. Then set up the case teams: four teams if the group is large enough, otherwise trios."},{"k":"section","n":"Case","h":"The HQ cleaning lot, end to end","sub":"Elena Ruiz wants a decision this month","note":"Transition: everything from today comes together on one decision. Remind them of the situation: Nordvia's office cleaning (P1, €2.4M, 3 sites in 2 countries) rolled over since 2019 with quality complaints at HQ; an RFP with a hybrid specification, a 3 + 1 + 1 term and a published 50/50 model; four bids for the HQ lot. They have already evaluated it in 3.3. About 25 minutes."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Case 3: your team's recommendation","min":12,"where":"Teams in breakout rooms · workbook Case 3","b":["How you handle Bidder C","Whom you negotiate with, or ask for a BAFO","Auction: yes or no, and why","How you treat D's request for a 4-year term","Your award recommendation and its conditions"],"note":"Open the rooms for 12 minutes; ask each team to name a spokesperson who will present in 90 seconds. Visit rooms and push back where teams want to auction B and D: two bidders, a labour-intensive service and a relationship that has to work for at least three years. On D's term, ask: ‘If you accept four years for D, what would B have bid on four years?’ Broadcast a three-minute warning, and remind them to answer the poll as soon as they come back."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s3-l4","note":"Each team votes once, through its spokesperson, or let everyone vote if teams were small. Show the results and use them to order the debrief: start with the team furthest from the majority. Do not reveal the strong answer yet."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Case debrief: what do we tell Elena Ruiz?","q":"The Head of Real Estate wants one name this month. What does your recommendation say, and what does it refuse to say?","b":["Bidder C: cleared, or rejected, with what evidence?","Why B and D, and why no auction?","D's 4-year term: deviation or deal-breaker?","How do you tell her the decision is close without sounding unsure?"],"min":13,"format":"Team spokespeople, then plenary","note":"Ninety seconds per spokesperson, then five minutes of open floor. The strong answer: ask C in writing to explain hours and price against the collective wage and reject it as abnormally low if not convincing, recording why; no auction, because after C there are only two strong bidders and the service is labour-intensive; a BAFO or negotiation with B and D only, on the published model; D asked to price the compliant 3 + 1 + 1 term, without changing the structure for one bidder; a recommendation that states the result is close (D leads by 0.1 at 50/50 without C; B leads with a linear formula), subject to due diligence and a mobilisation plan. On the last question: a close result stated with its sensitivity test is a sign of rigour, not weakness. Pitfall: teams that ‘fix’ the tie by changing weights or formula; call it out clearly. The award recommendation and the debrief of unsuccessful bidders come back in Session 4."},{"k":"two","h":"Session 3 and Fieldwork 3","l":{"t":"Session 3 in four lines","b":["The tool follows the strategy","Design the request so bids are comparable; fix the weights before issue","Run it fairly and test the sensitivity","Auction only when all six preconditions hold"]},"r":{"t":"Before Session 4","b":["RFx pack outline for your project","Weights validated with the business owner, before any bid","Three fictional bid sets through your model: does a ranking flip?","Tenant settings confirmed with your Coupa administrator","DORA or outsourcing gates agreed with Risk or Compliance, if flagged"]},"hi":"r","note":"Go through the right column and stress the weights conversation: ‘If the bids come in close, these weights decide. Are you comfortable with that?’ Showing the business owner a test bid set that reverses a ranking is the most convincing argument. Ask everyone to type in the chat the date of their meeting with the business owner, or the day they will book it. Then ask two or three people for the one thing they will do differently in their next event. Close with Session 4: HOW do we close it well, with negotiation planning, the Kelvar sole-source role-play, the contract in a regulated insurer and the handover. End on time."}],"polls":[{"id":"s3-l1","q":"The old RFx you audited for the pre-work: was a draft contract in the pack?","options":["Yes, with SLAs and KPIs","Yes, but without SLAs or regulatory annexes","No: we agreed the terms after award","We signed the supplier's own terms","I don't know"]},{"id":"s3-l2","q":"Elena Ruiz: ‘Four cleaning bids are in. Let's auction them and save another 10%.’ Your answer?","options":["Yes: English reverse among all four","Yes, but only among bidders above a quality threshold","Only after Bidder C has been cleared or excluded","No: clarify C, then BAFO with B and D on the published model"]},{"id":"s3-l3","q":"In a sourcing event today, what does your team really use Coupa for?","options":["Sending documents and receiving bids","Also Q&A in the Message Center","Also scoring and the award decision","End to end: award into CLM and a catalogue","We run most events outside Coupa"]},{"id":"s3-l4","q":"Case 3: your team's recommendation for the Nordvia HQ cleaning lot?","options":["Award to D now: highest score","Award to B now: better price, close score","Clarify C, then BAFO with B and D on the published model","Cancel and re-tender with a quality threshold","Auction between B and D"]}]}